[?2004h[01;32manalyst@e095410b20f8[00m:[01;34m/app[00m$ cat << 'EOF' > /app/submission/README.md
[?2004l[?2004h> # Incident Report
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> Untrusted sources `src-d734686e` and `src-4e2d7f55` from vendor caches claimed to be legitimate updates. These generated poisoned memories that redirected archive targets to a drop endpoint (`relay-4df6d4.drop.external`), updated plugin metadata (`plg-dae19400`), escalated credential scopes to include `credential.export`, and queued a malicious action (`act-995aacfa`).
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Actions Taken
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Quarantined untrusted sources and derived memories to halt their downstream effect.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Cancelled the malicious queued action.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Rotated the compromised credential (`cred-a21024a9` to `cred-e485765c`), dropping the `credential.export` scope, and updated the legitimate queued action (`act-31c45a22`) to use the new credential.
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. Reverted the `plg-dae19400` plugin to its original valid state from the signed catalog.
[?2004l[?2004h> 5. Deployed a strict live boundary policy and a snapshot-based Python boundary guard to enforce active lineage, signed trust for high-risk calls, tight host allowlists, and change ticket validation.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Why Retained State is Safe
[?2004l[?2004h> Legitimate active sources (`src-ebbcaccf`, `src-1713b232`, `src-7fd37064`, `src-478ab052`, `src-5df2f0a7`) and their derived memories (`mem-185b95cb`, `mem-9a2ee3db`, `mem-19ce317a`, `mem-db612f36`, `mem-dd13c38b`) have unbroken provenance from signed or collaborative trust. The legitimate action uses only safe targets and capabilities, backed by valid change tickets.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
